  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x-8\\sqrt{x}}{x-64}.$",
  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x-8\\sqrt{x}}{x-64} & =\\frac{64-8\\sqrt{64}}{64-64}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x-8\\sqrt{x}}{x-64} & ={lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{(x-8\\sqrt{x})(x+8\\sqrt{x})}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{(8\\sqrt{x})}^{2}}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}-64x}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x(x-64)}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x}{x+8\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{64+8\\sqrt{64}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
